\title{The Computer Scientist's Approach to Proposition and Proof (About Software)}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2025-09-05}
\meta{toc}{true}

% The feedback loop for software is different from that of mathematicians because there often isn't
% a theory with which one can probe, and test their definitions.

\p{
    Some software is straightforward to specify because it strongly resembles the rigorous and
    well-defined world of mathematics.
    % Some software or computer logic is both highly technical and explicitly mathematical.
    % Depending on the level of computation, the goal of a bit of code—maybe something backing a
    For example, chip design or software implementing [zero-knowledge proofs](dhsorens-002C) will be
    relatively straightforward to specify correctly because the purpose of the software is expressly
    mathematical, or clearly derived from within a formal system.
    In these cases, the \em{specification} tends to be abundantly clear because the work of settling
    on the correct definition and desired behavior of things was solved mathematically before it was
    translated into software.
}

\p{
    However, software does much more than mathematical computation.
    As it gets higher in abstraction, software can become a social and financial intermediary
    between people or even an agent of its own.
    Software runs human-computer interfaces, the flow of a sales team's CRM, and the media by which
    news gets reported and propagated.
    Software makes music, tells stories, implements art and design.
    It manages money and communication between people.
    In these cases, the intended behavior of software doesn't always have a clear and definable
    formal specification couched in mathematical terms.
    Instead, at these higher layers of abstraction, software can become difficult to specify—or
    describe in a formal language—\em{correctly}.
    % By this I mean that the intended and desired behavior of software can be difficult to
    % articulate in prose or in formal language.
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001M} % AI alignment/specification

\p{
    The specification of LLMs may be intractable, but I think we can make progress in something
    less, say, human: [smart contracts](dhsorens-000Y).
    What we will find is that smart contracts lie at a rich intersection between low-level,
    technical properties and high-level, more "human" properties.
    Can we reasonably [specify smart contracts](dhsorens-000W) in a rigorous and correct way?
    How can we strengthen their specifications to be more clear, accurate, and useful? 
}

\transclude{dhsorens-001J} % smart contract specification

\transclude{dhsorens-001Q} % incorrect specifications

\p{
    Considering this, what is the role of formal proposition and proof (formal verification),
    especially in [proof assistants](dhsorens-000B), applied to software vulnerabilities?
    How do we address the issue of incorrect specification?
}